 opportunities for people  who make drugs like heroin and fentanyl  to  expand their business and now a lot of  those users have transitioned over so we  are still losing some people to  prescription medications but mostly as  you said in your setup piece the deaths  are illicit drugs like fentanyl so it's  been a long uh a long horrible road for  us and i'm afraid there's just a lot  more to travel  and the region's hardest hit  are also places where we find the most  people who do not have health  insurance  politics and policies leading the poor  to take pills that kill them  do you agree with that is that an  accurate assessment of what's going on  it's absolutely the case that  these deaths are disproportionately  concentrated  in in areas where there's been  de-industrialization  where a lot of people do not have work  or have only low paying work and that is  part of the picture that has brought us  to this point but it certainly isn't all  of it it's also an incredible increase  in the supply beyond what has ever any  society has ever had before and those  two things together were deadly  combination for many americans  and  is that what explains america's overdose  epidemic dwarfing what we see here in  europe is it the supply issue  well we we certainly had far more supply  from the legal medication market us and  canada by the way which also has  epidemics so about that or interesting  increase  you do see something close to it in uh  scotland uh which has a more coming from  the illicit inherent supply but  it's we never never had as much exposure  in europe through the prescription  system also you know everybody in europe  has health insurance not everyone in  america does so there's people who want  treatment and can't get it that  exacerbates the the harm of being  addicted it's far more likely that  you'll  die um and also part of it's  geographical we are a very big country  we have a lot of people who live far  from hospitals far from help and if 